From: John Stringer <jcec_1@yahoo.co.uk>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 20:01:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 39/42] Paletted renderer: black pens get an entry of their own

Palette_MatchColour no longer matches a pen to entry 0. Entry 0 is kept
black for blanking (Render_PaletteIndexBlack), but a host may show it as
something else: on an Amiga colour 0 also fills the screen surround, and
Arnold for Amiga sets it to the border colour. A black pen matched to entry 0
was then drawn in the border colour, so black pixels vanished on a non-black
border (R-Type's Easter Egg logo lost its outline and letter shadows). Black
pens now take an entry of their own. Palette numbering changes only: RGB
output identical (headless checksums of the test set). NO_BLACK0_FIX restores
the old match (and, as before, drops the entry-0 reservation).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/cpc/render5.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cpc/render5.c b/src/cpc/render5.c
index bd4863e..c19e1ac 100644
--- a/src/cpc/render5.c
+++ b/src/cpc/render5.c
@@ -247,11 +247,20 @@ int Render_PaletteIndexBorder = 0;  /* border fix: palette index of pen 16 (see
 
 
 /* find a colour match */
+/* Amiga port (29 Oct 2026): entry 0 is never matched. Amiga colour 0 shows
+ * the border colour (host_amiga.c), so a black pen sharing entry 0 drew in the
+ * border colour: black pixels vanished on a grey border (R-Type's Easter Egg
+ * logo). Pens that are black get an entry of their own.
+ * NO_BLACK0_FIX: entry 0 is matched, as before. */
 int     Palette_MatchColour(int Red, int Green, int Blue)
 {
         int i;
 
+#ifndef NO_BLACK0_FIX
+        for (i=1; i<256; i++)
+#else
         for (i=0; i<256; i++)
+#endif
         {
                 if (CPCPalette[i].Flags & PALETTE_ENTRY_USED)
                 {
